WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Default credentials are especially dangerous in payment environments because attackers

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.380
routinely scan for known administrative paths,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.080
vendor defaults, sample accounts, and unmanaged service consoles.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.780
Open cloud storage, permissive access policies, public dashboards,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.200
and exposed payment administration tools can create direct paths to sensitive data or

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.700
systems that affect the cardholder data environment.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.560
Unnecessary services increase attack surface.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.660
Every open port, enabled plugin, feature flag, debug endpoint,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.640
and administrative function should have a documented purpose and owner.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.620
Debug configurations can disclose stack traces, secrets, paths, payment workflow details,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.680
and internal service names.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.980
Diagnostic detail belongs in protected logs, not public responses.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.840
Use secure configuration baselines for applications, cloud services, containers,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.460
and servers. Apply hardening standards and remove unnecessary functionality.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.760
Manage configuration changes through reviewed and repeatable processes.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.260
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:14.120
2.2 - Secure configuration standards; Req. 6.4 - Change management; Req.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.140
11.2 - Vulnerability scanning.
